WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.430 --> 00:00:13.211
was chopping wood for the cooking fires when Calypso came to him.

2
00:00:14.291 --> 00:00:15.631
"I have news," she said.

3
00:00:16.172 --> 00:00:19.112
Odysseus stopped chopping the wood and asked, "What news?"

4
00:00:19.132 --> 00:00:22.213
"It is time for you to leave," she replied.

5
00:00:22.233 --> 00:00:26.894
"Zeus' errand boy has spoken with me, and you are to make a raft and leave my island."

6
00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:28.775
"Leave on a raft?"

7
00:00:29.275 --> 00:00:30.475
answered Odysseus in dismay.

8
00:00:30.495 --> 00:00:32.456
"That would be suicide."

9
00:00:33.172 --> 00:00:37.554
"Zeus has commanded, and you and I must obey," she smiled scornfully and continued.

10
00:00:37.574 --> 00:00:40.395
"Hermes has assured me that you will be safe enough."

11
00:00:41.515 --> 00:00:46.477
Calypso turned and walked away, and Odysseus looked into the sky and asked, "Am I truly free?

12
00:00:47.038 --> 00:00:49.098
Give me a sign so that I may know it is true."

13
00:00:49.979 --> 00:00:53.260
Athena heard Odysseus and she sent her owl shooting through the sky.

14
00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:58.242
Odysseus saw the omen and knew that Athena had answered him, and joy burst from his heart.

15
00:00:59.363 --> 00:01:02.504
When Odysseus finished the raft, he and Calypso had a special meal.

16
00:01:03.476 --> 00:01:05.737
"I have grown fond of you," said Calypso.

17
00:01:05.757 --> 00:01:07.759
"I will miss you when you are gone."

18
00:01:08.519 --> 00:01:10.720
Odysseus thanked her and pushed his raft into the sea.

19
00:01:11.201 --> 00:01:16.204
For seventeen days Odysseus sailed uneventfully, but then Poseidon noticed him just as he sighted land.

20
00:01:16.724 --> 00:01:20.706
Poseidon, who was still angry with him, smashed his raft with a terrible storm.

21
00:01:21.287 --> 00:01:24.709
The raft shattered and Odysseus clung to a log while the tempest raged.

22
00:01:25.289 --> 00:01:29.152
The next morning Odysseus awoke and, as he was lying on the sand, he could hear voices in

23
00:01:29.172 --> 00:01:29.672
the distance.

24
00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:34.921
Odysseus carefully searched for the source of these voices and he found that several young maidens were doing

25
00:01:34.941 --> 00:01:35.982
their laundry in a stream.

26
00:01:36.962 --> 00:01:40.543
When Odysseus announced himself all the maidens ran away except for Nausicaa.

27
00:01:40.563 --> 00:01:42.243
"Are you a beggar or a thief?"

28
00:01:42.563 --> 00:01:43.004
she asked.

29
00:01:43.944 --> 00:01:45.964
"I am neither," replied Odysseus.

30
00:01:45.984 --> 00:01:47.985
"Are you a goddess or a nymph?

31
00:01:48.205 --> 00:01:49.865
For you are too beautiful to be mortal."

32
00:01:50.686 --> 00:01:53.126
The princess giggled and replied, "I am neither.

33
00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:56.947
I am Nausicaa, the daughter of Alcinous, the king."

34
00:01:57.447 --> 00:02:03.138
Odysseus bowed to the princess and said, "Beautiful maiden, take me to your father, for I have a

35
00:02:03.219 --> 00:02:05.580
sad story to tell, and a favor to ask."

36
00:02:06.401 --> 00:02:11.144
Nausicaa looked at Odysseus and said, "I will take you to my father, but first you must wash.

37
00:02:11.685 --> 00:02:13.106
I will wait over there with my maids."

38
00:02:13.626 --> 00:02:18.830
When Odysseus looked into the still pool, he was shocked by his gruesome features—his clothes were ripped, torn,

39
00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:22.853
and stained with blood, his face was bruised, swollen, and covered with grime.

40
00:02:23.333 --> 00:02:26.696
He looked like a lunatic, and he was amazed that Nausicaa hadn't run away.

41
00:02:27.900 --> 00:02:33.282
Nausicaa took Odysseus to her father, and after he heard Odysseus' story he gave Odysseus both gold and

42
00:02:33.342 --> 00:02:34.222
passage by ship.

43
00:02:34.622 --> 00:02:36.282
The ship took Odysseus back to Ithaca.

44
00:02:36.763 --> 00:02:40.544
When he arrived home he could barely recognize it because the island was covered in fog.

45
00:02:41.104 --> 00:02:44.605
As soon as he stepped off the ship, he dropped to his knees and kissed the earth.

46
00:02:45.205 --> 00:02:48.606
Tears filled his eyes as he remembered the twenty long years that he had been away.

47
00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:52.967
"Rise, Odysseus, and welcome home," said a voice out of the mist.

48
00:02:53.487 --> 00:02:55.748
Odysseus stood and found himself in front of Athena.

49
00:02:56.716 --> 00:02:58.938
"There is trouble in your house," she continued.

50
00:02:58.958 --> 00:03:01.880
"A large group of suitors have come to take your wife's hand.

51
00:03:02.661 --> 00:03:03.902
They dishonor you and your son.

52
00:03:04.382 --> 00:03:05.323
You must take revenge."

53
00:03:05.343 --> 00:03:08.265
"Great goddess, how should I strike them?"

54
00:03:09.066 --> 00:03:09.746
asked Odysseus.

55
00:03:09.766 --> 00:03:14.410
"You will know how, when the time is right, but first you must learn who has been loyal

56
00:03:14.450 --> 00:03:16.011
to you and who has been disloyal.

57
00:03:16.631 --> 00:03:19.293
I will conceal your identity until it is time for your revenge."

58
00:03:19.554 --> 00:03:22.336
But before Odysseus could reply, the goddess was gone.

59
00:03:23.808 --> 00:03:26.611
The first person that Odysseus met was the swineherd Eumaeus.

60
00:03:27.271 --> 00:03:31.955
Eumaeus told Odysseus about the trouble that the suitors had caused; eating all the food, drinking all the

61
00:03:31.995 --> 00:03:34.738
wine, and slaughtering the herds like there was a feast every night.

62
00:03:34.758 --> 00:03:36.960
"Why doesn't the master of the house stop this?"

63
00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:38.161
asked Odysseus.

64
00:03:38.181 --> 00:03:41.464
"It is because the gods have cursed him.

65
00:03:42.024 --> 00:03:46.448
They have kept him away from home for a long time, but his son is still loyal and

66
00:03:46.508 --> 00:03:47.749
so are many of the servants."

67
00:03:48.950 --> 00:03:53.741
Odysseus looked at the swineherd and said, "I believe you to be one of those loyal servants, and

68
00:03:53.821 --> 00:03:54.361
I bless you."

69
00:03:55.141 --> 00:03:59.842
While Odysseus was speaking with the swineherd, Athena found Telemachus, Odysseus' son.

70
00:03:59.862 --> 00:04:02.583
"Your father will soon return," she said.

71
00:04:02.603 --> 00:04:05.484
"Find the swineherd, and prepare for his arrival."

72
00:04:05.984 --> 00:04:10.325
Telemachus did not recognize the goddess, but he saw a falcon strike a dove, and he knew that

73
00:04:10.365 --> 00:04:11.666
the stranger had spoken the truth.

74
00:04:12.686 --> 00:04:14.986
Telemachus immediately went in search of the swineherd.

75
00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:16.487
He found the swineherd at home.

76
00:04:16.507 --> 00:04:20.288
"Loyal Eumaeus, I have news," called Telemachus.

77
00:04:20.936 --> 00:04:22.917
"Soon our lord and my father will return.

78
00:04:23.557 --> 00:04:24.477
Go and tell the others."

79
00:04:24.497 --> 00:04:28.598
"I knew he would come back," said the swineherd.

80
00:04:28.618 --> 00:04:32.799
"I'll wait for your return," said Telemachus, "so be quick."

81
00:04:33.179 --> 00:04:34.580
The swineherd rushed off at once.

82
00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.600
Inside the hut Athena had appeared.

83
00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.541
"Odysseus, it is time.

84
00:04:39.101 --> 00:04:40.561
Stand and be ready to greet your son."

85
00:04:41.422 --> 00:04:45.763
When Telemachus opened the door to the swineherd's hut, there was a blinding flash of light.

86
00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:47.243
"What god are you?"

87
00:04:47.883 --> 00:04:49.584
asked Telemachus when he saw his father.

88
00:04:50.192 --> 00:04:53.295
"I am no god," answered Odysseus.

89
00:04:53.315 --> 00:04:54.356
"I am your father."

90
00:04:54.976 --> 00:05:01.282
Telemachus at first doubted Odysseus' claim and then Athena said, "Do not doubt, noble Telemachus, for there is

91
00:05:01.322 --> 00:05:02.043
much work to do."

92
00:05:02.063 --> 00:05:06.587
"Yes, my son, it is time to plan our revenge, and I will need your help if we

93
00:05:06.647 --> 00:05:08.648
are to succeed," said Odysseus.

94
00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.192
Telemachus returned home and prepared for his father's revenge.

95
00:05:12.792 --> 00:05:14.694
First, he gathered up and hid all the weapons.

96
00:05:15.254 --> 00:05:16.936
He then told his mother to have a contest.

97
00:05:17.408 --> 00:05:21.932
The suitor who could string Odysseus' bow and shoot an arrow through twelve hoops could marry her.

98
00:05:22.652 --> 00:05:27.016
Athena again placed Odysseus in disguise, and he went with the swineherd to his home.

99
00:05:27.596 --> 00:05:30.679
The suitors were feasting when Odysseus and the swineherd arrived.

100
00:05:31.239 --> 00:05:37.384
The suitors treated Odysseus the beggar and the swineherd badly, and in anger Odysseus said, "You who feast

101
00:05:37.445 --> 00:05:40.547
on another's food, are unwilling to spare a crust of bread for me.

102
00:05:41.128 --> 00:05:42.248
You are worse than thieves!"

103
00:05:42.949 --> 00:05:47.600
The suitors were angered by the beggar's harsh words, but before they could punish him, Penelope arrived.

104
00:05:47.620 --> 00:05:50.321
"I have made a decision," she said.

105
00:05:50.341 --> 00:05:55.623
"My lord and husband will never return, so I will marry the man who can string my husband's

106
00:05:55.663 --> 00:05:58.343
bow and shoot an arrow through twelve golden hoops."

107
00:05:59.284 --> 00:06:01.884
Each suitor approached the bow and each suitor failed to string it.

108
00:06:01.904 --> 00:06:04.285
"Is there no one among you as good as my husband?"

109
00:06:04.905 --> 00:06:06.205
called Penelope to the suitors.

110
00:06:06.225 --> 00:06:12.187
"Mother," interrupted Telemachus, "there may be one here among these men who can, but I doubt if the

111
00:06:12.227 --> 00:06:13.588
suitors will allow him to try.

112
00:06:14.168 --> 00:06:18.603
He is my guest, and so he shall try in my name, for a son cannot compete for

113
00:06:18.643 --> 00:06:19.443
his mother's hand."

114
00:06:19.944 --> 00:06:22.365
Telemachus snatched the bow and handed it to the beggar.

115
00:06:22.826 --> 00:06:24.467
The beggar took the bow and quickly strung it.

116
00:06:24.927 --> 00:06:29.351
A loud clap of thunder boomed in the distance as Odysseus fitted an arrow to the string and

117
00:06:29.411 --> 00:06:30.091
raised the bow.

118
00:06:30.872 --> 00:06:36.576
Seemingly without aiming, Odysseus let the arrow fly and it unerringly passed through each of the twelve hoops.

119
00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:40.599
A flash of lightning shot across the sky and the beggar was transformed.

120
00:06:40.619 --> 00:06:42.000
"I have returned!"

121
00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:46.582
shouted Odysseus as he fitted arrows to his bow and slew the suitors where they stood.

122
00:06:46.602 --> 00:06:48.283
"And this is my due."

123
00:06:48.964 --> 00:06:53.406
Telemachus and the loyal servant also joined the fight, and soon all the suitors and those disloyal to

124
00:06:53.526 --> 00:06:54.727
Odysseus were slain.

125
00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:59.789
"The battle is won," said Odysseus to his son, "but our home is befouled.

126
00:07:00.190 --> 00:07:03.191
Let the servants clean the house before we celebrate my homecoming."

127
00:07:04.392 --> 00:07:09.375
Once the remains from the battle had been cleaned away, Odysseus presented himself formally to his wife, Penelope.

128
00:07:10.155 --> 00:07:14.378
She was still uncertain about his identity, so she quizzed him about their bedroom and the bed that

129
00:07:14.398 --> 00:07:15.099
they had shared.

130
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:20.685
Odysseus described how he had made it out of a single piece of cedar, and Penelope rejoiced because

131
00:07:20.725 --> 00:07:22.587
her husband had finally returned.
